{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "pqcscan",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/cartterrr/pqcscan",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "py.rsa-keygen",
              "name": "RsaKeyGeneration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RSA key generation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "It isn't clear from this line whether the key encrypts or signs. If it encrypts or does key exchange, treat it as urgent (hybrid ML-KEM); if it signs, it can wait for ML-DSA. Also note where the key is stored and how long the data it protects needs to stay secret."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "It isn't clear from this line whether the key encrypts or signs. If it encrypts or does key exchange, treat it as urgent (hybrid ML-KEM); if it signs, it can wait for ML-DSA. Also note where the key is stored and how long the data it protects needs to stay secret."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "cryptography",
                  "post-quantum",
                  "quantum-key"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.rsa-encrypt",
              "name": "RsaEncryptionOaepPkcs1V15",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RSA encryption (OAEP / PKCS#1 v1.5)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Anything encrypted this way can be recorded now and decrypted later, once a quantum computer exists. Switch key exchange to a hybrid of X25519 and ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203). In TLS that means enabling the X25519MLKEM768 group, which OpenSSL 3.5+ and Go 1.24+ support. If the app wraps data keys with RSA-OAEP or ECDH itself, use a hybrid KEM there instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Anything encrypted this way can be recorded now and decrypted later, once a quantum computer exists. Switch key exchange to a hybrid of X25519 and ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203). In TLS that means enabling the X25519MLKEM768 group, which OpenSSL 3.5+ and Go 1.24+ support. If the app wraps data keys with RSA-OAEP or ECDH itself, use a hybrid KEM there instead."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "cryptography",
                  "post-quantum",
                  "quantum-kex"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.x25519",
              "name": "X25519X448KeyAgreement",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "X25519 / X448 key agreement"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Anything encrypted this way can be recorded now and decrypted later, once a quantum computer exists. Switch key exchange to a hybrid of X25519 and ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203). In TLS that means enabling the X25519MLKEM768 group, which OpenSSL 3.5+ and Go 1.24+ support. If the app wraps data keys with RSA-OAEP or ECDH itself, use a hybrid KEM there instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Anything encrypted this way can be recorded now and decrypted later, once a quantum computer exists. Switch key exchange to a hybrid of X25519 and ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203). In TLS that means enabling the X25519MLKEM768 group, which OpenSSL 3.5+ and Go 1.24+ support. If the app wraps data keys with RSA-OAEP or ECDH itself, use a hybrid KEM there instead."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "cryptography",
                  "post-quantum",
                  "quantum-kex"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "any.private-key",
              "name": "PrivateKeyEmbeddedInFile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Private key embedded in file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A private key is checked into the repo. Rotate it, scrub it from git history, and load keys from a secret manager or HSM from now on."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A private key is checked into the repo. Rotate it, scrub it from git history, and load keys from a secret manager or HSM from now on."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "cryptography",
                  "post-quantum",
                  "secret"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "key.ssh-public-key",
              "name": "SshPublicKey",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSH public key"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A quantum computer could forge these signatures, but it can't go back and break old ones, so this can usually wait until key exchange is done. The replacement is ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204), or SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) for long-lived roots and firmware signing. Reading the algorithm from config makes the switch much easier later."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A quantum computer could forge these signatures, but it can't go back and break old ones, so this can usually wait until key exchange is done. The replacement is ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204), or SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) for long-lived roots and firmware signing. Reading the algorithm from config makes the switch much easier later."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "cryptography",
                  "post-quantum",
                  "quantum-sig"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
